  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and tranquil weather is expected to continue\nthrough this Monday.\n\nAloft, a high pressure ridge builds from the Upper Midwest today\nand settles over the Northeast United States and vicinity,\nincluding our CWA, tonight through Sunday night. At the surface,\nthe ridge continues to affect Western and Central NY as the\nembedded high pressure center wobbles generally S'ward from \ncentral QC to the Mid-Atlantic states. Dry weather persists in\nour CWA as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the ridge.\n\nClear to mainly clear sky is expected through most of the time \nperiod. However, isolated to scattered lake-effect stratocumuli \nover and downwind of the ~16C eastern Great Lakes are expected \nto persist through this late morning as a sufficiently-cold mean\nlow-level flow veers from N'erly to ENE'erly. Lake-induced CAPE\nwill remain limited as synoptic-scale low-level moisture \nremains scarce. These stratocumuli should dissipate by midday as\ndaytime heating of surrounding land and overlying air causes \nthe eastern Great Lakes to no longer be the primary source and \nwarmth, moisture, and instability. Scattered to widespread \nupslope stratocumuli over the Western Southern Tier and vicinity\nshould dissipate by daybreak this morning in response to the \nveering mean low-level flow and continued low-level dry air \nadvection. The lake-effect and upslope stratocumuli are expected\nto remain too shallow to produce precip. Otherwise, variable \namounts of mid/upper-level cloudiness associated with moist \nisentropic ascent preceding shortwave trough axes embedded in \nthe NW'erly to W'erly flow aloft are expected, especially \nduring today's mid-morning hours through the first few predawn \nhours of Saturday morning and Sunday afternoon through Sunday \nnight.\n\nLows should reach mainly the upper 20's to upper 30's around\ndaybreak this morning and be followed by highs in the 50's to\nlower 60's late this afternoon as weak low-level CAA continues\nin the wake of Thursday's cold front passage. Tonight's lows\nshould reach mainly the mid 20's to mid 30's around daybreak\nSaturday as clearing sky, weak or calm surface winds, and low\nhumidity promote efficient radiational cooling. Late afternoon\nhighs should reach the mid 50's to lower 60's on Saturday as\nweak WAA begins to develop along the western flank of the low-\nlevel ridge. Overnight lows mainly in the mid 20's to upper \n30's are forecast around daybreak Sunday due to another round \nof efficient radiational cooling. Warmer highs, mainly in the \nupper 50's to upper 60's, are expected on Sunday and should be \nfollowed by overnight lows in the mid 30's to upper 40's around \ndaybreak Monday as the WAA regime strengthens somewhat along the\nwestern flank of the low-level ridge.\n\nNote: Clear sky or sufficient cloud breaks and weak or calm\nsurface winds should contribute to localized steam fog in the \nBlack River Valley, Western Southern Tier river valleys, and the\nlake valleys of the Finger Lakes Region during the predawn \nhours through about 9 AM EDT today through Monday, respectively.\n\nOn Monday, cyclonic W'erly to WNW'erly flow aloft and embedded \nshortwave troughs are expected to impact our region. Variable\namounts of mainly mid/upper-level cloudiness are expected amidst\nmoist isentropic ascent ahead of the shortwave trough axes. At \nthe surface, the aforementioned ridge will exit generally \nSE'ward and be followed by the SE'ward passage of a weak cold \nfront during the late morning through early afternoon. A rather \ndry low-level atmospheric column should permit a dry cold front \npassage. Behind the front, another high pressure ridge should \nbuild from central Canada and vicinity through the evening. Late\nafternoon highs should reach mainly the mid 50's to mid 60's.",
